The model compiles cleanly. Final state of the deliverable:

**Object** — a folded cloth (two stacked linen layers) authored y-up/+z-forward, then scaled/positioned with `fit_to_box` into the 0.24 × 0.03 × 0.18 box.

- **`cloth_base`** (root, `lower_fold`): a closed thin-shell cloth sheet built as a procedural grid mesh — rounded squircle planform, ~1.2 mm thin selvedge tapering to a ~5.5 mm thick middle, undulating underside resting on the ground, multi-frequency wrinkle field on top, plus a rounded fold roll bulging just inside the back edge (the crease), which is what makes it read as a turned-over fold rather than a pancake.
- **`cloth_flap`** (child, `upper_fold`): the second, slightly smaller layer lying on the lower one. Its underside is generated from the same surface field as the base top plus a gap that is pressed closed at the crease and at the free front edge and puffed up in the middle, so it reads as loose cloth conforming to the fold; its top carries its own higher-amplitude wrinkles and a few soft lumps. Geometry is authored in world space and rebased into the hinge frame.
- **Articulation** `base_to_flap`: revolute about the crease line at the back edge, `axis=(-1,0,0)` so positive q lifts the free (+z) edge, limits 0 → 1.6 rad, effort 2.0, velocity 1.2. The crease edge lies exactly on the joint axis, so the layer stays attached through the whole swing.
- **Materials**: warm cream linen for the upper fold, slightly darker shadowed linen for the lower fold.
- **Tests**: scoped `allow_overlap` for the hidden pressed cloth interface (proved by exact `expect_contact`, `expect_within`, `expect_overlap` in the folded pose, plus a contact check at the fully unfolded pose and a check that the free edge actually rises > 4 cm when opened).

Rest-pose extents measure 0.240 × 0.025 × 0.179 m, correctly centered inside the constraint box.

